[THOUGHT] The provisional diagnosis of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is clinically well-supported by systemic B symptoms, widespread lymphadenopathy with fixed/nontender nodes, splenomegaly, elevated LDH, and CD20+ lymphoid infiltrate with high proliferative activity. However, the diagnosis remains *histopathologically presumptive* — definitive classification requires integration of morphology, full IHC profiling (e.g., cell-of-origin: GCB vs. non-GCB via Hans algorithm), and critical molecular data. Most critically:  
- Ki-67 index is pending — essential to quantify proliferation and support high-grade behavior;  
- FISH for *MYC*, *BCL2*, and *BCL6* is pending — indispensable to rule out double- or triple-hit lymphoma (DHL/THL), which carries distinct prognosis and mandates intensified therapy (e.g., DA-EPOCH-R);  
- Bone marrow biopsy is not yet ordered — required for accurate Ann Arbor staging (Stage IV if involved);  
- PET/CT is not yet performed — gold standard for initial staging and baseline metabolic assessment; without it, disease extent (e.g., extranodal involvement, metabolic burden) remains incompletely defined.  
These omissions are not ancillary — they directly impact diagnostic subclassification, risk stratification (IPI and NCCN-IPI), therapeutic selection, and prognostication. At Turn 7/10, time-sensitive staging and biomarker workup remains incomplete, and delaying these risks inappropriate treatment assignment.

[DECISION] INCOMPLETE

[FEEDBACK] The diagnosis cannot be finalized as "Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma" without: (1) confirmed histopathologic diagnosis (morphology + full IHC panel including CD10, BCL6, MUM1, BCL2, p53); (2) Ki-67 index ≥80% to corroborate high proliferative activity; (3) FISH results for *MYC*, *BCL2*, and *BCL6* to exclude double-/triple-hit lymphoma; (4) bone marrow biopsy result; and (5) PET/CT scan for accurate staging. All five are mandatory before definitive diagnosis and treatment planning.

[THOUGHT] The supervisor has explicitly identified five mandatory missing elements:  
(1) Full IHC panel (CD10, BCL6, MUM1, BCL2, p53) — needed for cell-of-origin classification (GCB vs. non-GCB) and risk stratification;  
(2) Ki-67 index ≥80% — required to confirm high proliferative activity consistent with aggressive lymphoma;  
(3) FISH for MYC/BCL2/BCL6 — to rule out double-/triple-hit lymphoma;  
(4) Bone marrow biopsy — essential for staging (Ann Arbor stage IV if involved);  
(5) PET/CT — gold standard for metabolic staging and identifying extranodal involvement.  

We have only CD20+ so far. Among these, the *bone marrow biopsy* is both minimally invasive, highly specific for lymphoma staging, and yields definitive evidence of systemic dissemination — which directly impacts Ann Arbor staging (e.g., stage IV if positive) and treatment intensity. It is also a prerequisite before initiating curative-intent therapy. Since it’s one of the five mandated items and hasn’t been addressed yet, it’s the most appropriate next test.

[TEST] Bone marrow biopsy with aspirate and trephine core, including flow cytometry and histopathology.

